"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:06,831\nThe entorhinal cortex: the brain\u2019s grid\n\n3\n00:00:09,831 --> 00:00:16,304\nIn 2005, May-Britt and Edvard Moser and their team recorded cells next to the hippocampus,\n\n4\n00:00:16,530 --> 00:00:19,271\nin a region called the entorhinal cortex.\n\n5\n00:00:19,727 --> 00:00:21,178\nAs a rat explored,\n\n6\n00:00:21,395 --> 00:00:23,417\none cell fired not in one place,\n\n7\n00:00:23,437 --> 00:00:24,364\nbut in many.\n\n8\n00:00:24,814 --> 00:00:26,412\nWhen they marked those places,\n\n9\n00:00:26,539 --> 00:00:28,817\nthey formed a grid of triangles.\n\n10\n00:00:28,987 --> 00:00:30,712\nThey called them grid cells.\n\n11\n00:00:31,006 --> 00:00:34,156\nEach one fires at the corners of a repeating pattern,\n\n12\n00:00:34,343 --> 00:00:37,099\nas if the floor were tiled with triangles.\n\n13\n00:00:37,438 --> 00:00:39,826\nNothing on the floor is marked that way.\n\n14\n00:00:40,249 --> 00:00:42,299\nThe brain makes the grid itself.\n\n15\n00:00:43,269 --> 00:00:45,833\nGrid cells come in sets of different sizes,\n\n16\n00:00:46,027 --> 00:00:48,236\nfrom fine grids to coarse ones.\n\n17\n00:00:48,688 --> 00:00:52,084\nOne grid alone can't tell you exactly where you are,\n\n18\n00:00:52,325 --> 00:00:54,166\nbecause its pattern repeats.\n\n19\n00:00:54,538 --> 00:00:58,104\nCombining sets of different sizes pins down your position.\n\n20\n00:00:59,574 --> 00:01:02,055\nGrid cells work with other cells nearby.\n\n21\n00:01:02,437 --> 00:01:04,961\nHead direction cells act like a compass.\n\n22\n00:01:05,322 --> 00:01:07,587\nBorder cells fire near walls.\n\n23\n00:01:07,850 --> 00:01:12,183\nTogether, this system can keep track of where you are from your own movements,\n\n24\n00:01:12,371 --> 00:01:13,411\neven in the dark.\n\n25\n00:01:13,964 --> 00:01:16,877\nLandmarks then correct the errors that build up.\n\n26\n00:01:18,047 --> 00:01:20,208\nPeople seem to have grid cells too.\n\n27\n00:01:20,722 --> 00:01:29,108\nIn 2010, brain scans of people moving through a virtual world showed the six-fold pattern that grid cells produce.\n\n28\n00:01:29,543 --> 00:01:33,894\nThose with the clearest pattern were best at remembering where objects were.\n\n29\n00:01:36,364 --> 00:01:42,185\nThe entorhinal cortex is one of the first regions affected by Alzheimer's disease.\n\n30\n00:01:42,597 --> 00:01:47,148\nThat may be why getting lost in a familiar place can be an early sign.\n\n31\n00:01:48,918 --> 00:01:50,548\nWhy does this matter for learning?\n\n32\n00:01:51,033 --> 00:01:52,969\nIn a 2016 study,\n\n33\n00:01:53,161 --> 00:01:58,653\nthe same grid pattern appeared while people learned about birds that varied in two features.\n\n34\n00:01:59,113 --> 00:02:01,531\nThe grid may help organize ideas,\n\n35\n00:02:01,615 --> 00:02:02,776\nnot just places.\n\n36\n00:02:03,112 --> 00:02:05,800\nSo try laying new ideas out like a map,\n\n37\n00:02:06,153 --> 00:02:08,009\nby how they relate to each other.\n\n38\n00:02:09,979 --> 00:02:12,763\nThe entorhinal cortex measures out space.\n\n39\n00:02:13,074 --> 00:02:15,894\nWith the hippocampus, it tells you where you are.\n\n"